course: dictionary
lesson: 1559
title: Words that repeat
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A chant is words said or sung again and again in a strong rhythm, often by a crowd. Look for the clues: the same words come back, and they have a beat, like a drum. «Mill Lane, Mill Lane, can't play a game!» is a chant that the Rovers' parents shout at a football match.
explain: Chant can also be a noun for a simple tune used in church to sing prayers. Context tells you which meaning is right. «Karakia are Māori chants or prayers, often used to open gatherings in New Zealand» is about prayers, not football. When you read chant, ask: is the text about a crowd, a school, or a church?
explain: Chant is also a verb, which means to say or sing words again and again in a rhythm. Children chant times tables so that they remember them. Try your meaning in the sentence: if «chant» means "say again and again in a rhythm", does the sentence still make sense? Then it is the right meaning.
fact: A chant is words repeated over and over in a rhythm.
fact: «Chant» can be a noun (a football chant) or a verb (to chant).
fact: A Gregorian chant is a simple church tune used to sing prayers or psalms.
fact: «Karakia» (New Zealand) are Māori chants or prayers, often used to open gatherings.
fact: Children chant times tables to help them learn them by repeating.
exercise: «Mill Lane, Mill Lane, can't play a game!» is an example of what? | A kind of football boot | A painted holy picture | A quiet whisper in a library | *Words repeated in a rhythm
exercise: In school, children chant the times tables. Why do they do this? | To go to sleep | To hide the answers from their friends | To make the teacher cross | *To help them learn them by repeating
exercise: «Karakia are Māori chants or prayers.» Which country do karakia come from? | *New Zealand | Canada | Scotland | Australia
test: In church, a chant is a simple tune used to sing what? | *Prayers or psalms | Shopping lists | Football scores | Weather reports
test: What does «sitter» mean in «Sam misses a sitter»? | A rest after running | A seat in the stands | A person who looks after children | *An easy chance to score
exam: Which sentence uses chant as a verb, meaning to say words again and again in a rhythm? | The chant was heard across the stadium | *The crowd began to chant the same words over and over | The chant was printed on the back of the programme | The chant was sung in church on Sunday
